                %li
                  %a{:href => "#open_string_worldsheet_anomaly_cancellation"} Open string worldsheet Anomaly cancellation
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_dirac_structures"} Relation to Dirac structures
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An abstractly defined
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is a consistent assignment of
            = succeed "-space" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %em open-closed QFT
            the cobordisms are allowed to have
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+CFT"} boundaries
          %p
            In this abstract formulation of QFT a
            %strong D-brane
            is a type of data assigned by the QFT to boundaries of cobordisms.
          %p
            For a broader perspective see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} brane
          %h3#in__rational_cft
            In
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2d
            rational CFT
          %p
            A well understood class of examples is this one: among all 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            that case of
            %em full rational 2d CFT
            has been understood completely, using
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FFRS-formalism"} FFRS-formalism
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+category"} modular tensor category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                (to be thought of as being the category of representations of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
                of the 2d CFT);
            %li
              %p
                a special symmetric
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+algebra"} Frobenius algebra
                object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
                to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            In this formulation a type of
            %strong brane
            of the theory is precisely an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            (an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodule"} bimodule
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bi-brane"} bi-brane
            or
            %em defect line
            ):
          %p
            the 2d cobordisms with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+CFT"} boundary
            on which the theory defined by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathcal{C}
            carry as extra structure on their connected boundary pieces a label given by an equivalence class of an
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %ul
            %li
              %p triangulating the cobordism,
            %li
              %p
                labeling all internal edges by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %li
              %p
                labelling all boundary pieces by the
                = succeed "-module" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %li
              %p all vertices where three internal edges meet by the multiplication operation
            %li
              %p
                and all points where an internal edge hits a boundary by the corresponding
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                morphism
            %li
              %p
                and finally evaluating the resulting
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            So in this abstract algebraic formulation of QFT on the worldvolume, a brane is just the datum assigned by the QFT to the boundary of a cobordism. But abstractly defined QFTs may arise from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+model"} sigma model
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/BraneAndOpenString.jpg", :width => "700"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#IbanezUranga12"} Ibanez-Uranga 12
          %h3#in__tft
            In
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2d
            TFT
          %p
            Another case where the branes of a QFT are under good mathematical control is
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            = succeed "-version" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
          %p
            Particularly the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            are well understood.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the branes of the B-model (“B-branes”) form the the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} stable (infinity,1)-category
                of
                = succeed "es" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaves"} quasicoherent sheaves
                on the target space (often considered just in terms of its
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} homotopy category of an (infinity,1)-category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
                of quasicoherent sheaves);
            %li
              %p
                the branes of the A-model form the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fukaya+category"} Fukaya category
                of the target space.
            %li
              %p
                the category of D-branes of the A-model on a symplectic
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau-Ginzburg+model"} Landau-Ginzburg model
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fukaya-Seidel+category"} Fukaya-Seidel category
            %li
              %p
                the category of D-branes of the B-model on a complex Landau-Ginzburg model is a category of
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+factorization"} matrix factorization
          %p
            There is also a mathematical structure called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+topology"} string topology
            with D-branes. At present this is more “string inspired” than actually derived from string theory, though.
          %h3#in_terms_of_geometric_data_of_the_model_background
            In terms of geometric data of the
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %p
            An abstractly defined QFT (as a consistent assignment of state spaces and propagators to cobordisms as in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            from
            %em geometric data
            \:
          %p
            Such a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
              QFT
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            on a space of maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \to X
            from a cobordism (“worldvolume”)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            to some target space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that may carry further geometric data such as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
          %p
            One may therefore try to match the geometric data on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that encodes the
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            that results after quantization. This gives a geometric interpretation to many of the otherwise purely abstract algebraic properties of the worldvolume QFT.
          %p
            It turns out that if one checks which geometric data corresponds to the
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %em submanifolds
            of the target space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            data.
          %p
            A well-understood case is the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wess-Zumino-Witten+model"} Wess-Zumino-Witten model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a simple
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = G
            and the background field is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle 2-bundle with connection
            (a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbe"} bundle gerbe
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
          %p
            In this case it turns out that branes for the sigma model on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are given in the simplest case by conjugacy classes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \subset G
            inside the group, and that these carry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+bundle"} twisted vector bundle
            with the twist given by the Kalb-Ramond background bundle. These vector bundles are known in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            literature as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chan-Paton+vector+bundles"} Chan-Paton vector bundles
            \. The geometric intuition is that a QFT with certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+CFT"} boundary condition
            comes from a quantization of spaces of maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \to G
            that are restricted to take the boundary of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            to these submanifolds.
          %p
            More generally, one finds that the geometric data that corresponds to the branes in the algebraically defined 2d QFT is given by cocycles in the twisted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential K-theory
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            The case of rational 2d CFT considered so far is only the best understood of a long sequence of other examples. Here the collection of all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            – identified with the collection of all internal modules over an internal frobenius algebra, forms an ordinary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
          %p
            More generally, at least for 2-dimensional
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
            of boundary condition objects. For instance, for what is called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            2-d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+sheaves"} coherent sheaves
            on some Calabi-Yau space.
          %p
            Starting with Kontsevich’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            reformulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+mirror+symmetry"} mirror symmetry
            the study of (derived) D-brane categories has become a field in its own right in pure mathematics.
          %p … lots of further things to say …
          %h3#AsBlackBranes As black branes
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+coupling+constant"} string coupling constant
            the D-branes are incarnated as boundary conditions for the string’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            increases and becomes non-perturbative, this picture of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            breaks down, but at low energy (large scales) now
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            becomes a good description, and now the D-branes are incarnated as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+branes"} black branes
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/BlackBrane.png"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#IbanezUranga12"} Ibanez-Uranga 12
          %p
            This transition is also the key to understanding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+holes+in+string+theory"} black holes in string theory
          %div
            %p
              %strong>
                1/2
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BPS+state"} BPS
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+branes"} black branes
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F1-brane"} F1-brane
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NS5-brane"} NS5-brane
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            %p
              %img{:src => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/BraneSolutionsInSupergravity.jpg", :width => "600"}/
            %blockquote
